  [34mNotification[39m  { id, taskId, kind, title, firedAt, readAt|[34mnull[39m }

  Two fields deserve their reasoning stated, since both are places where I've made a judgment call you may want to
  overturn.

  [1m[38;5;153mwatched[39m.[0m Your request said "tasks they care about," and this is what makes that explicit rather than guessed. My
  proposal: [38;5;153mwatched[39m defaults to [38;5;153mtrue[39m for any task that has a [38;5;153mdueAt[39m, and the user can mute a task without clearing its
  deadline. The alternative — treating every task with a due date as implicitly watched, no flag — is one less field and
  one less checkbox. I'd keep the flag, because "I want the deadline recorded but stop nagging me" is a real state, and
  retrofitting it later means migrating stored data.

  [1m[38;5;153mkind[39m.[0m Exactly two reminder kinds: [38;5;153mupcoming[39m (crossing the lead-time threshold) and [38;5;153moverdue[39m (past [38;5;153mdueAt[39m). One of each
  per task, enforced by the ledger. I deliberately did not include recurring reminders, snooze, or per-task custom lead
  times. Each is defensible on its own and all three together turn a 300-line app into a scheduling engine. Snooze is
  the one I'd expect you to want first; it's a clean later addition — a [38;5;153msnoozedUntil[39m field and one extra rule clause —
  so I'd rather leave it out now than build it speculatively.

  [1mLead time[0m is a single global setting, default 15 minutes, stored alongside the inbox.

  [1mPersistence[0m is write-through on every mutation, with reads tolerant of malformed JSON: a parse failure logs and starts
  from empty rather than throwing at module load. Storage is versioned with a [38;5;153mschemaVersion[39m key so a later shape change
  has somewhere to hook a migration.
